-- title:
--   Theorem 3.1 — DPP for the IQ function: $Q(\mu,h)=\widehat r(\mu,h)+\gamma\sup_{h^\prime\in\mathcal H}Q(\Phi(\mu,h),h^\prime)$ (corrected: $Q$ is not claimed measurable)
-- statement:
--   Let $(P,R,\gamma)$ be a mean-field control model with learning: a representative agent's state evolves by $s_{t+1}\sim P(s_t,\mu_t,a_t,\nu_t)$, it plays $a_t\sim\pi_t(s_t,\mu_t)$ under a relaxed feedback control $\pi$, and it receives a stochastic reward $r_t\sim R(s_t,\mu_t,a_t,\nu_t)$, where $\mu_t$ and $\nu_t$ are the laws of $s_t$ and $a_t$ (the McKean–Vlasov dynamics (2.7)). For $\mu\in\mathcal P(\mathcal S)$ and a local policy $h\in\mathcal H$ let
--   $$Q(\mu,h)=\sup_{\pi\in\Pi_1}\mathbb E^\pi\Big[\sum_{t=0}^\infty\gamma^t r(s_t,\mu_t,a_t,\nu_t)\,\Big|\,s_0\sim\mu,\ a_0\sim h(s_0),\ a_t\sim\pi_t(s_t,\mu_t),\ t\in\mathbb N\Big]$$
--   be the integrated Q (IQ) function (3.4).
--
--   **Theorem.** Under Outstanding Assumption (A), for any $\mu\in\mathcal P(\mathcal S)$ and $h\in\mathcal H$,
--   $$Q(\mu,h)=\widehat r(\mu,h)+\gamma\sup_{h'\in\mathcal H}Q\big(\Phi(\mu,h),h'\big),$$
--   where $\Phi(\mu,h)$ (2.11) is the next state distribution and $\widehat r(\mu,h)$ (2.13) the integrated reward.
--
--   This is the Bellman equation for mean-field control with learning on the lifted state space $\mathcal P(\mathcal S)$ and action space $\mathcal H$; it is the basis of Q-learning for mean-field control.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $Q^\pi(\mu,h)$ is an expectation under the canonical path law of (2.7), not the sum $\sum_t\gamma^t\widehat r(\mu_t,h_t)$ (that identity is Lemma 2.2). Definition 3.1 describes $Q$ as "a measurable real-valued function"; measurability is not part of the statement.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--
--   - correction: Definition 3.1: "the IQ function is a measurable real-valued function defined on P(S) × H" → Q is a real-valued function on P(S) × H; no measurability is asserted; reason: measurability is false for uncountable S under every σ-algebra on H: S = [0,1], A = {0,1}, γ = 1/2, P(s,µ,a,ν) = δ_a, R(s,µ,a,ν) = δ_a satisfy every assumption of the source (|r| ≤ 1 gives (A)); for Borel B ⊆ S and h_B(s) = δ₁ if s ∈ B, δ₀ otherwise, Φ(δ_s,h_B)({1}) = r̂(δ_s,h_B) = 1_B(s) and Q(δ_s,h_B) = 1 + 1_B(s), so joint measurability under any σ-algebra Σ on H would make {(s,h_B) : s ∈ B} measurable in Borel(S) ⊗ Σ, which a diagonal argument (Aumann 1961) rules out; neither Theorem 3.1 nor its proof uses it
--   - encoding: H = {h : S → P(A) measurable such that Outstanding Assumption (A) holds} (2.9) → H = all Markov kernels S → A (LocalPolicy); reason: (A) is a standing assumption over all of Π and every measurable h is the constant control π_t ≡ h ∈ Π, so the side condition excludes nothing
--   - encoding: Π₁ = controls {π_t}_{t≥1} starting at time 1 → an ℕ-indexed Control whose t-th kernel plays at time t + 1 (Control.cons, Control.shift); reason: the model is time-homogeneous; the re-indexing is a bijection and the paper itself uses sup_{π∈Π₁} v^π = v (p.10)
--   - reading: (2.7) gives the one-step laws of s_{t+1}, a_t and r_t and leaves the joint conditional law of (r_t, s_{t+1}) given (s_t, a_t) open → the canonical path law draws r_t and s_{t+1} conditionally independently given (s_t, a_t); reason: every expectation in the paper depends only on the laws of (s_t, a_t, r_t), which Lemmas 2.1–2.2 show are the same for every realization of (2.7)
--   - generalization: S, A compact separable metric spaces with their Borel σ-fields; P(X) with W₁ and its Borel σ-field (pp.3–4) → S, A arbitrary measurable spaces; P(X) = ProbabilityMeasure X with the Giry σ-algebra; reason: no metric or compactness is used; the paper identifies the Borel σ-field of P(X) with the σ-field generated by µ ↦ µ(C) (p.3), which is the Giry σ-algebra, so the compact Borel case is an instance
--   - encoding: Outstanding Assumption (A): for any s₀ = ξ ∼ µ, sup_{π∈Π} E^π[Σ_t γ^t |r(s_t, µ_t, a_t, ν_t)|] < ∞ → for every µ, the supremum over controls of the lintegral of Σ_t γ^t |r_t| under the canonical path law is < ⊤; reason: a lintegral of a nonnegative series is the expectation without any integrability side condition; the expectation is the same for every realization of (2.7) (Lemmas 2.1–2.2), and the bound is per µ, as printed
--   - encoding: E^π[Σ_{t=0}^∞ γ^t r(s_t, µ_t, a_t, ν_t)] → Bochner integral of ω ↦ ∑' t, γ^t r_t(ω) under the canonical path law; reason: under (A) the series converges absolutely almost surely and is integrable (Lemma 2.2), so ∑' is the sum of the series and the integral is the expectation
-- source:
--   Gu, Guo, Wei, Xu, Dynamic Programming Principles for Mean-Field Controls with Learning, arXiv:1911.07314v6, Theorem 3.1, (3.5), p.9; Definition 3.1, (3.4), p.9

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_MFCLearn_Model
import Definitions.Def_MFCLearn_Lifted
import Definitions.Def_MFCLearn_Process

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

namespace MFCLearn

/-- **Theorem 3.1 (DPP for the IQ function).** Gu, Guo, Wei, Xu, *Dynamic Programming Principles for
Mean-Field Controls with Learning*, arXiv:1911.07314v6, Theorem 3.1, (3.5), p.9: "Under Outstanding
Assumption (A), for any μ ∈ P(S) and h ∈ H, Q(μ, h) = r̂(μ, h) + γ sup_{h′∈H} Q(Φ(μ, h), h′)."

**Formalization Note** `Q` is the IQ function (3.4), the supremum over `π ∈ Π₁` of the expected
discounted reward under the canonical path law of the McKean–Vlasov dynamics (2.7) that plays `h` at
time 0. Definition 3.1 also calls `Q` measurable; that claim is not part of the statement (it is false
for uncountable `S` for every σ-algebra on `H`, and the theorem does not use it). -/
theorem theorem_3_1 {S A : Type*} [MeasurableSpace S] [MeasurableSpace A]
    (m : MFCModel S A) (hA : m.OutstandingA) (μ : ProbabilityMeasure S) (h : LocalPolicy S A) :
    m.Q μ h = m.rhat μ h + m.γ * ⨆ h' : LocalPolicy S A, m.Q (m.Phi μ h) h' := by sorry

end MFCLearn
